/* BULIG Level 7 · Genuine Love for Reading and Writing (Grade 7 · campus journalism, Division of Bukidnon). */
window.M = window.M || {}; M.HUB_L7 = [{"n":1,"title":"Scrambled Facts and Information","kind":"write","info":["News is an account of an event, or a fact or an opinion that interests people.","It is a presentation of current events.","The Inverted Pyramid Style When you write a story in inverted pyramid format, you put the most newsworthy information at the beginning of the story and the least newsworthy information at the end. The inverted pyramid helps readers scan news stories quickly and efficiently. But it helps you write news stories quickly and efficiently, too. Once you train yourself to organize facts this way, you can apply this formula to almost any breaking news event — which is why the inverted pyramid has been a cornerstone of newswriting for the past century."],"prompt":["An announcement was made School Principal Dr. Mary Jean M.","Francisquete on Monday that a new program will be implemented by the school.","The Special Program in Journalism (SPJ) will be implemented by Badas National High School for school year 2021-2022.","After the implementation, the school is expected to receive equipment and facilities related to the resources needed for SPJ. “We are happy that SPJ will be implemented in Badas NHS because it will give our children the chance to be better writers and journalists in the future,” Linda Jesus, one of the parents said.","The aim of implementing the program is to offer journalism competencies to cater the journalistic skills of students in the school.","Francisquete said that the SPJ implementation became a reality after parents and other stakeholders wrote a letter to the school to open the said program.","“We want to give our students the chance to hone their writing skills and abilities in journalism and also make sure that we can influence them to love writing,” Francisquete said.","Instruction/s: Write a news story of 5 to 7 paragraphs using the structure below. The scrambled information is attached below. THE LEAD. (Who did what; or What happened? Perhaps When? and where? might be in this paragraph.)"],"example":[],"sections":[{"l":"The lead","h":"Who did what; what happened? Maybe when and where.","rows":3},{"l":"2nd paragraph","h":"Why? How?","rows":3},{"l":"3rd paragraph","h":"A quote keeps the story lively.","rows":3},{"l":"4th paragraph","h":"Why does this matter? (significance)","rows":3},{"l":"5th paragraph","h":"What will other people think? (human context)","rows":3},{"l":"6th paragraph","h":"How did the event continue? (background)","rows":3},{"l":"7th paragraph","h":"What happens next?","rows":3}],"rub":[["Content and ideas",4],["Organization",4],["Language and style",4],["Mechanics (spelling, grammar, punctuation)",4]]},{"n":2,"title":"News Article","kind":"write","info":["News is an account of an event, or a fact or an opinion that interests people.","It is a presentation of current events.","The Inverted Pyramid Style When you write a story in inverted pyramid format, you put the most newsworthy information at the beginning of the story and the least newsworthy information at the end. The inverted pyramid helps readers scan news stories quickly and efficiently. But it helps you write news stories quickly and efficiently, too. Once you train yourself to organize facts this way, you can apply this formula to almost any breaking news event — which is why the inverted pyramid has been a cornerstone of newswriting for the past century."],"prompt":["Write a news article based on the following facts.","What: 1. government troops on the hunt for Abu Sayyaf chieftain Khadaffy Janjalani and the alleged masterminds of the 2002 Bali bombings clashed with a group of bandits.","2. three marines were injured after the incident","Where: in the Southern island province of Sulu When : on Friday This was according to military reports.","Supporting details:","Lieutenant Colonel Arnel Caculitan - Marines spokesman.","Around 11:30 am, troops from the 7th Marine Batallion clashed with some 40 Abu Sayaff members. The clashed happened in Danag Village. This was in Patikul town. This was according to Caculitan.","Caculitan further said that the Abu Sayaff suffered casualties. He cannot give exact figure. He also said that He could not easily ascertain if Janjalani and two other Jimaah Islamiya bomb experts Dulmatin and Omar Patik were with the group that engaged with the marines.","Sulu province – has been sealed off in the hope of catching the Al Qaeda- linked militants. The Abu Sayaff and the JI have been responsible for the worst terrorists attack in South east Asia.","The Al-Qaeda militants have escaped military dragnet several times. The United States has put up ten million dollar bounty. This was for Dulmatin’s capture. One million dollar each for the capture of Janjalani and Patek."],"example":[],"sections":[{"l":"Headline","h":"Short, present tense, no period.","rows":1},{"l":"News article","h":"Inverted pyramid: most important facts first.","rows":12}],"rub":[["Content and ideas",4],["Organization",4],["Language and style",4],["Mechanics (spelling, grammar, punctuation)",4]]},{"n":3,"title":"News Writing Activity","kind":"write","info":["News is an account of an event, or a fact or an opinion that interests people.","It is a presentation of current events.","The Inverted Pyramid Style When you write a story in inverted pyramid format, you put the most newsworthy information at the beginning of the story and the least newsworthy information at the end. The inverted pyramid helps readers scan news stories quickly and efficiently. But it helps you write news stories quickly and efficiently, too. Once you train yourself to organize facts this way, you can apply this formula to almost any breaking news event — which is why the inverted pyramid has been a cornerstone of newswriting for the past century."],"prompt":["Make a news article based on the following facts:","Who: National Bureau of Investigation and Bangko Sentral ng Pilipinas What:","They arrested a counterfeiter. They also seized hundreds of fake denomination Philippine peso bills.","When:","The arrest happened last week.","Where: It happened in Sta Cruz, Manila.","Other details:","-- Nestor Mantaring – NBI Officer in charge.","-- He presented the counterfeiter in a press conference on Monday afternoon.","-- Ronnie Jacobo – the counterfeiter.","-- He was a resident of 895 Quezon Boulevard, Sta Cruz, Manila. -- Article 166 is all about forging treasury or bank notes, obligations and securities.","-- Jacobo faces charges of violating Article 166.","-- Six to twelve years imprisonment – this will be the punishment of Jacobo if proven guilty.","-- Marivic Jacobo – Ronnie’s wife. She is an accomplice to the crime. The NBI is also searching for Marivic.","-- Regional Director Ruel Lasala – head of the NBI National Capital Region Division. He launched an investigation upon receiving information that the suspect was engaged in the printing and sale of fake 500 and 1,000 peso bills.","-- An NBI agent posed as a buyer and bought a fake 500 and 1,000 peso bills from Ronnie and Marivic.","-- Wilfredo Villaluz – Bangko Sentral ng Pilipinas Supervising Agent. According to him, the suspect did a good job.","-- Bills were complete with watermark and security thread.","-- Upon closer look one would notice that the security thread is actually a sticker.","-- The suspect (Jacobo) admitted using Corel draw and Adobe Photoshop basic graphic design program for his counterfeiting activity. -- The 500 peso bills and the 1,000 peso bills were sold at 75 each. -- 453,000 – the amount of counterfeit bills recovered by the authorities."],"example":[],"sections":[{"l":"Headline","h":"Short, present tense, no period.","rows":1},{"l":"News article","h":"Inverted pyramid: most important facts first.","rows":12}],"rub":[["Content and ideas",4],["Organization",4],["Language and style",4],["Mechanics (spelling, grammar, punctuation)",4]]},{"n":4,"title":"Is Social Media Helping or Harming Students Today?","kind":"write","info":["EDITORIAL WRITING","• They usually appear on the editorial or opinion page to differentiate","them from news stories.","• Editorials comment on issues reported in the news articles.","Weighing and presenting arguments, an editorial reaches a conclusion and makes a recommendation regarding an issue.","• The editorial is the official stand of a newspaper. Because of these","they are written in such a way that it carries the opinion of the whole publication","• “I” and “my” are not used because they indicate personal","opinion of a single writer.","• The editorial always includes a title.","PARTS OF EDITORIAL WRITING","1. Introduction – It is the beginning paragraph. It may contain the","news upon which the editorial is based, in which in case, the introduction is called. You state as briefly as possible the background and topic Last week the Parents Teachers Association (PTA) decided to close the school cafeteria because the place remained dirty despite repeated calls for cleaning up. Thrash left from lunches, used bottles and plastic cans were found on tables, chairs, floors. Even left-over food was piled to rot in a corner. PTA members had complained about these in the past to no avail.","2. Body - This contains the facts about the news or issues including","the writer’s opinion or viewpoints. It builds up the case through a logical arrangement of ideas supported by facts Reaction – sets the reader up to receive the opinion The PTA decision was correct and necessary. Not only does the dirty cafeteria cause health problems to students, it gives a bad reflection of the school as well.","Details – support your reaction and lead to a conclusion The problem surfaced several months ago when the PTA inspected the school laboratories and facilities. The PTA members found the cafeteria messy and unclean that time.","After the inspection, Mr. Rene Ramirez, the PTA president, held a dialog with the cafeteria manager and appealed for the maintenance of canteen cleanliness.","The canteen became clean for about a month. After that, the place went back to its old dirty state.","3. Conclusion- This is the last part either summarizes or drives home","the point. It ties up the ideas and arguments presented in the body. This is sometimes known as clincher.","We support the closing of the canteen in the hope that when it opens again, a cleaner, safer cafeteria will serve the school. More than our support however, we believe that maintaining the cafeteria clean and safe is everybody’s duty. It is not the duty of the management alone, not the duty of the PTA alone, but the duty of all of us.","Last week the Parents Teachers Association (PTA) decided to close the school cafeteria because the place remained dirty despite repeated calls for cleaning up. Thrash left from lunches, used bottles and plastic cans were found on tables, chairs, floors. Even left-over food was piled to rot in a corner. PTA members had complained about these in the past to no avail.","The PTA decision was correct and necessary. Not only does the dirty cafeteria cause health problems to students, it gives a bad reflection of the school as well.","The problem surfaced several months ago when the PTA inspected the school laboratories and facilities. The PTA members found the cafeteria messy and unclean that time.","After the inspection, Mr. Rene Ramirez, the PTA president, held a dialog with the cafeteria manager and appealed for the maintenance of canteen cleanliness.","The canteen became clean for about a month. After that, the place went back to its old dirty state.","We support the closing of the canteen in the hope that when it opens again, a cleaner, safer cafeteria will serve the school. More than our support however, we believe that maintaining the cafeteria clean and safe is everybody’s duty. It is not the duty of the management alone, not the duty of the PTA alone, but the duty of all of us."],"prompt":["Topic: Is social media helping or harming students today?","Write 3–4 sentences giving your opinion. State your opinion clearly, give one reason to support your opinion, and end with a short, convincing sentence."],"example":["I think social media is harming students today. Many students spend too much time on apps and games, which can make them tired and distracted from their studies. For example, some students scroll through social media instead of doing homework. Students should use social media less so it does not affect their learning."],"sections":[{"l":"Your opinion (3–4 sentences)","h":"State your opinion · one reason · a convincing ending.","rows":6}],"check":["I stated my opinion clearly.","I gave one reason to support it.","I ended with a short, convincing sentence."],"rub":[["Content and ideas",4],["Organization",4],["Language and style",4],["Mechanics (spelling, grammar, punctuation)",4]]},{"n":5,"title":"Short Editorial","kind":"write","info":["EDITORIAL WRITING","• They usually appear on the editorial or opinion page to differentiate","them from news stories.","• Editorials comment on issues reported in the news articles.","Weighing and presenting arguments, an editorial reaches a conclusion and makes a recommendation regarding an issue.","• The editorial is the official stand of a newspaper. Because of these","they are written in such a way that it carries the opinion of the whole publication","• “I” and “my” are not used because they indicate personal","opinion of a single writer.","• The editorial always includes a title.","PARTS OF EDITORIAL WRITING","1. Introduction – It is the beginning paragraph. It may contain the","news upon which the editorial is based, in which in case, the introduction is called. You state as briefly as possible the background and topic Last week the Parents Teachers Association (PTA) decided to close the school cafeteria because the place remained dirty despite repeated calls for cleaning up. Thrash left from lunches, used bottles and plastic cans were found on tables, chairs, floors. Even left-over food was piled to rot in a corner. PTA members had complained about these in the past to no avail.","2. Body - This contains the facts about the news or issues including","the writer’s opinion or viewpoints. It builds up the case through a logical arrangement of ideas supported by facts Reaction – sets the reader up to receive the opinion The PTA decision was correct and necessary. Not only does the dirty cafeteria cause health problems to students, it gives a bad reflection of the school as well.","Details – support your reaction and lead to a conclusion The problem surfaced several months ago when the PTA inspected the school laboratories and facilities. The PTA members found the cafeteria messy and unclean that time.","After the inspection, Mr. Rene Ramirez, the PTA president, held a dialog with the cafeteria manager and appealed for the maintenance of canteen cleanliness.","The canteen became clean for about a month. After that, the place went back to its old dirty state.","3. Conclusion- This is the last part either summarizes or drives home","the point. It ties up the ideas and arguments presented in the body. This is sometimes known as clincher.","We support the closing of the canteen in the hope that when it opens again, a cleaner, safer cafeteria will serve the school. More than our support however, we believe that maintaining the cafeteria clean and safe is everybody’s duty. It is not the duty of the management alone, not the duty of the PTA alone, but the duty of all of us.","Last week the Parents Teachers Association (PTA) decided to close the school cafeteria because the place remained dirty despite repeated calls for cleaning up. Thrash left from lunches, used bottles and plastic cans were found on tables, chairs, floors. Even left-over food was piled to rot in a corner. PTA members had complained about these in the past to no avail.","The PTA decision was correct and necessary. Not only does the dirty cafeteria cause health problems to students, it gives a bad reflection of the school as well.","The problem surfaced several months ago when the PTA inspected the school laboratories and facilities. The PTA members found the cafeteria messy and unclean that time.","After the inspection, Mr. Rene Ramirez, the PTA president, held a dialog with the cafeteria manager and appealed for the maintenance of canteen cleanliness.","The canteen became clean for about a month. After that, the place went back to its old dirty state.","We support the closing of the canteen in the hope that when it opens again, a cleaner, safer cafeteria will serve the school. More than our support however, we believe that maintaining the cafeteria clean and safe is everybody’s duty. It is not the duty of the management alone, not the duty of the PTA alone, but the duty of all of us."],"prompt":["Write a short editorial (1 paragraph).","Start with a strong opinion (agree or disagree).","Give two reasons to support your viewpoint.","Provide one possible solution (e.g., limit, guidance).","End with a closing statement that urges readers to agree with you."],"example":["Social media is both helping and harming students, but I believe it is mostly harmful. It can help students communicate with friends and learn new things, but many spend too much time online, which affects their schoolwork and sleep. For example, students may stay up late watching videos instead of studying for tests. To reduce these problems, students should set limits on their social media use and focus on important tasks first. Schools and parents can also guide students to use social media responsibly. Using social media in moderation can help students enjoy its benefits without hurting their studies."],"sections":[{"l":"Title","h":"","rows":1},{"l":"Editorial (1 paragraph)","h":"Strong opinion · two reasons · one solution · closing that urges readers.","rows":9}],"check":["Strong opinion","Two reasons","One possible solution","Closing statement"],"rub":[["Content and ideas",4],["Organization",4],["Language and style",4],["Mechanics (spelling, grammar, punctuation)",4]]},{"n":6,"title":"Editorial Writing Activity","kind":"write","info":["EDITORIAL WRITING","• They usually appear on the editorial or opinion page to differentiate","them from news stories.","• Editorials comment on issues reported in the news articles.","Weighing and presenting arguments, an editorial reaches a conclusion and makes a recommendation regarding an issue.","• The editorial is the official stand of a newspaper. Because of these","they are written in such a way that it carries the opinion of the whole publication","• “I” and “my” are not used because they indicate personal","opinion of a single writer.","• The editorial always includes a title.","PARTS OF EDITORIAL WRITING","1. Introduction – It is the beginning paragraph. It may contain the","news upon which the editorial is based, in which in case, the introduction is called. You state as briefly as possible the background and topic Last week the Parents Teachers Association (PTA) decided to close the school cafeteria because the place remained dirty despite repeated calls for cleaning up. Thrash left from lunches, used bottles and plastic cans were found on tables, chairs, floors. Even left-over food was piled to rot in a corner. PTA members had complained about these in the past to no avail.","2. Body - This contains the facts about the news or issues including","the writer’s opinion or viewpoints. It builds up the case through a logical arrangement of ideas supported by facts Reaction – sets the reader up to receive the opinion The PTA decision was correct and necessary. Not only does the dirty cafeteria cause health problems to students, it gives a bad reflection of the school as well.","Details – support your reaction and lead to a conclusion The problem surfaced several months ago when the PTA inspected the school laboratories and facilities. The PTA members found the cafeteria messy and unclean that time.","After the inspection, Mr. Rene Ramirez, the PTA president, held a dialog with the cafeteria manager and appealed for the maintenance of canteen cleanliness.","The canteen became clean for about a month. After that, the place went back to its old dirty state.","3. Conclusion- This is the last part either summarizes or drives home","the point. It ties up the ideas and arguments presented in the body. This is sometimes known as clincher.","We support the closing of the canteen in the hope that when it opens again, a cleaner, safer cafeteria will serve the school. More than our support however, we believe that maintaining the cafeteria clean and safe is everybody’s duty. It is not the duty of the management alone, not the duty of the PTA alone, but the duty of all of us.","Last week the Parents Teachers Association (PTA) decided to close the school cafeteria because the place remained dirty despite repeated calls for cleaning up. Thrash left from lunches, used bottles and plastic cans were found on tables, chairs, floors. Even left-over food was piled to rot in a corner. PTA members had complained about these in the past to no avail.","The PTA decision was correct and necessary. Not only does the dirty cafeteria cause health problems to students, it gives a bad reflection of the school as well.","The problem surfaced several months ago when the PTA inspected the school laboratories and facilities. The PTA members found the cafeteria messy and unclean that time.","After the inspection, Mr. Rene Ramirez, the PTA president, held a dialog with the cafeteria manager and appealed for the maintenance of canteen cleanliness.","The canteen became clean for about a month. After that, the place went back to its old dirty state.","We support the closing of the canteen in the hope that when it opens again, a cleaner, safer cafeteria will serve the school. More than our support however, we believe that maintaining the cafeteria clean and safe is everybody’s duty. It is not the duty of the management alone, not the duty of the PTA alone, but the duty of all of us."],"prompt":["Write a well-developed editorial (2 paragraphs).","Paragraph 1: Present your opinion clearly. Explain one positive and one negative effect of social media.","Paragraph 2: State which effect you think is stronger and why. Provide evidence or examples (realistic, simple ones). End with a strong call to action (e.g., encouraging balanced or responsible use)."],"example":["Social media is a powerful tool that can both help and harm students today. On the positive side, it allows students to connect with classmates, share ideas, and access educational content online. For example, students can join study groups or watch tutorials on difficult topics. However, social media can also distract students, cause stress, and reduce the time they spend on homework or outdoor activities. I believe the negative effects are stronger than the positive ones if social media is not used carefully. Students should be taught to use social media responsibly and set daily time limits. Parents and teachers can also encourage activities like reading, sports, or hobbies to balance screen time. By using social media wisely, students can enjoy its benefits without letting it harm their health or learning."],"sections":[{"l":"Title","h":"","rows":1},{"l":"Paragraph 1","h":"Your opinion · one positive and one negative effect.","rows":7},{"l":"Paragraph 2","h":"Which effect is stronger and why · evidence · call to action.","rows":7}],"rub":[["Content and ideas",4],["Organization",4],["Language and style",4],["Mechanics (spelling, grammar, punctuation)",4]]},{"n":7,"title":"Sports Writing: Game Recap","kind":"write","info":["Sports Writing is a form of journalism that reports on sports topics and events. While the sports department within some newspapers has been mockingly called the toy department, because sports journalists do not concern themselves with the 'serious' topics covered by the news desk, sports coverage has grown in importance as sport has grown in wealth, power and influence.","HOW TO WRITE A SPORTS WRITING:","1. Have a strong lead. Like any article, you want to start your sports","article out with a strong lead, one that encapsulates the available information on \"who, what, where, when, why and how.\" This is basic for how to write articles of any topic. Look at sports articles in almost any paper to see how they introduce the game or event they are covering with their lead paragraph. They get the reader’s attention with a strong but concise summary of the story to follow.","2. Write clearly and concisely. If you've ever read Sports Illustrated,","you know that some sports articles can be what you might call \"literary non-fiction:\" lengthy, poetic, filled with metaphor and digressions into back story. If your particular assignment requires that kind of writing, go for it. But if you read the daily sports section of your city paper, you will also notice that most sports articles reporting on the sporting events of the past day are concisely written. Yes, sports articles include context and metaphor and technical sports terms, but they're also to-the-point and generally stick to basic vocabulary. Being specific is one of the more basic tips for article writing.","3. Know the context. Like any news article, how to write a sports","article will require you to have a basic working knowledge of the universe on which you're reporting. This may mean not only knowing all about the current players, coaches and standings but knowing some history, as well. This may be common knowledge to you, but if not, you may need to do some research.","You will also need to know about the sport itself including rules, history, league standings, current controversies and other information. You may already know much of this if you're a sports enthusiast--but be aware that a journalist may need more in-depth knowledge than a casual fan. Be sure to have a solid grasp on the sport you're covering before you start to cover it.","Also, keep in mind that many sports teams have press departments that will provide journalists with extensive information about their organizations including current players and team history."],"prompt":["Imagine (or choose) a game Write a short recap including:","your school team played.","1. Who played","2. What sport","3. The final score","4. One highlight moment","5. One sentence about how the team felt afterward","This helps students build simple sports-story structure."],"example":[],"sections":[{"l":"Headline","h":"","rows":1},{"l":"Game recap","h":"Who played · what sport · final score · one highlight · how the team felt.","rows":8}],"check":["Who played","What sport","Final score","One highlight moment","How the team felt"],"rub":[["Content and ideas",4],["Organization",4],["Language and style",4],["Mechanics (spelling, grammar, punctuation)",4]]},{"n":8,"title":"Sports Writing: Quotes from an Interview","kind":"write","info":["Sports Writing is a form of journalism that reports on sports topics and events. While the sports department within some newspapers has been mockingly called the toy department, because sports journalists do not concern themselves with the 'serious' topics covered by the news desk, sports coverage has grown in importance as sport has grown in wealth, power and influence.","HOW TO WRITE A SPORTS WRITING:","1. Have a strong lead. Like any article, you want to start your sports","article out with a strong lead, one that encapsulates the available information on \"who, what, where, when, why and how.\" This is basic for how to write articles of any topic. Look at sports articles in almost any paper to see how they introduce the game or event they are covering with their lead paragraph. They get the reader’s attention with a strong but concise summary of the story to follow.","2. Write clearly and concisely. If you've ever read Sports Illustrated,","you know that some sports articles can be what you might call \"literary non-fiction:\" lengthy, poetic, filled with metaphor and digressions into back story. If your particular assignment requires that kind of writing, go for it. But if you read the daily sports section of your city paper, you will also notice that most sports articles reporting on the sporting events of the past day are concisely written. Yes, sports articles include context and metaphor and technical sports terms, but they're also to-the-point and generally stick to basic vocabulary. Being specific is one of the more basic tips for article writing.","3. Know the context. Like any news article, how to write a sports","article will require you to have a basic working knowledge of the universe on which you're reporting. This may mean not only knowing all about the current players, coaches and standings but knowing some history, as well. This may be common knowledge to you, but if not, you may need to do some research.","You will also need to know about the sport itself including rules, history, league standings, current controversies and other information. You may already know much of this if you're a sports enthusiast--but be aware that a journalist may need more in-depth knowledge than a casual fan. Be sure to have a solid grasp on the sport you're covering before you start to cover it.","Also, keep in mind that many sports teams have press departments that will provide journalists with extensive information about their organizations including current players and team history."],"prompt":["Interview a friend or teammate about a game or practice. Ask questions like:","“What was the hardest part of the game?”","“What made you proud?”","“What do you want to improve?”","Then write three short sentences, each starting with a quote from the person."],"example":["“‘I was nervous at first,’ Maya said.”"],"sections":[{"l":"Quote 1","h":"Start with a quote: “I was nervous at first,” Maya said.","rows":2},{"l":"Quote 2","h":"","rows":2},{"l":"Quote 3","h":"","rows":2}],"rub":[["Content and ideas",4],["Organization",4],["Language and style",4],["Mechanics (spelling, grammar, punctuation)",4]]},{"n":9,"title":"Sports Writing: Game Coverage","kind":"write","info":["Sports Writing is a form of journalism that reports on sports topics and events. While the sports department within some newspapers has been mockingly called the toy department, because sports journalists do not concern themselves with the 'serious' topics covered by the news desk, sports coverage has grown in importance as sport has grown in wealth, power and influence.","HOW TO WRITE A SPORTS WRITING:","1. Have a strong lead. Like any article, you want to start your sports","article out with a strong lead, one that encapsulates the available information on \"who, what, where, when, why and how.\" This is basic for how to write articles of any topic. Look at sports articles in almost any paper to see how they introduce the game or event they are covering with their lead paragraph. They get the reader’s attention with a strong but concise summary of the story to follow.","2. Write clearly and concisely. If you've ever read Sports Illustrated,","you know that some sports articles can be what you might call \"literary non-fiction:\" lengthy, poetic, filled with metaphor and digressions into back story. If your particular assignment requires that kind of writing, go for it. But if you read the daily sports section of your city paper, you will also notice that most sports articles reporting on the sporting events of the past day are concisely written. Yes, sports articles include context and metaphor and technical sports terms, but they're also to-the-point and generally stick to basic vocabulary. Being specific is one of the more basic tips for article writing.","3. Know the context. Like any news article, how to write a sports","article will require you to have a basic working knowledge of the universe on which you're reporting. This may mean not only knowing all about the current players, coaches and standings but knowing some history, as well. This may be common knowledge to you, but if not, you may need to do some research.","You will also need to know about the sport itself including rules, history, league standings, current controversies and other information. You may already know much of this if you're a sports enthusiast--but be aware that a journalist may need more in-depth knowledge than a casual fan. Be sure to have a solid grasp on the sport you're covering before you start to cover it.","Also, keep in mind that many sports teams have press departments that will provide journalists with extensive information about their organizations including current players and team history."],"prompt":["Write a sports coverage using the following facts.","What:","Municipal Meet volleyball eliminations Where:","Arayata Sports Complex, Tanza, Cavite When:","September 9 at 2 p.m.","Between:","Saint John School (SJS) and Saint Mark Academy (SMA) Scores: 25-19, 23-25, 25-20, 25-20 Significance of the game:","SJS on 2-0 lead; routed Saint Joseph Institute (SJI) in previous outing Heroes of the game: Jerome Arbis and Noli Cruz combined for 17 points in last set Quotes from SJS coach Rody Santos:","“My boys are pumped up from their victory over the Saint Joseph Institute Tossers.”","“This is a good start as we are gunning for the championship like our basketball varsity posting a 6-0 winning streak.”","Weather condition:","Heavy rains and thunderstorms brought by Typhoon Maring but the Arayata gym was filled to the rafter.","Spectators’ attitude:","SJS crowd favorite, predominately SJS crowd chanting cheers; SJS red and white clad mascot dancing in front of SJS contingent Reported injury:","SMA’s Jin Rivas sprained ankle after a bad fall in the second set; carried out of playing court and brought to Family General Hospital Highlights of the game:","SMA came on strong in second set with Joseph Rom’s mounting strong wallops Rom’s heroics aided by Dan Sibal’s stopping ball on a service by SJS Richard Montes, also in second set."],"example":[],"sections":[{"l":"Headline","h":"","rows":1},{"l":"Sports coverage","h":"Lead · game highlights · quotes · injury · crowd.","rows":12}],"rub":[["Content and ideas",4],["Organization",4],["Language and style",4],["Mechanics (spelling, grammar, punctuation)",4]]},{"n":10,"title":"Copyreading 1: Influenza-like Cases","kind":"copy","info":["Copyreading is editing or correcting errors made by the reporter and proofreading the errors made by the typesetter or printer.","It is the art of arranging, correcting, and selecting the quality and type of news It is also called copyediting.","One who edits copies is called a copyreader or copyeditor What to copyread?","1. Errors in fact","2. Errors in grammar","3. Errors in structure","4. Errors in style","5. Libelous and derogatory statements","6. Seditious and rebellious matter","7. Opinion, editorializing statements in news","8. Verbal deadwoods, redundancy","9. Errors in unity, coherence and emphasis","PROCEDURE IN COPYREADING","1. Read the whole article first to determine what kind of story it is.","2. Having determined the type of story, read the article again. This","time determine what the lead paragraph is.","3. Take note of errors in fact. Check on the accuracy of the","statements and information. Verify when in doubt.","4. Read the story again and mark all paragraph beginnings. Then,","correct errors in grammar, spelling, punctuations, and style using copyreading symbols.","5. Improve the organization of the story. Make sure the lead features","the lead fact.","6. Go over the whole story and again. Remove all unnecessary and","irrelevant materials.","7. Check the length of the story and then prepare the clean copy.","8. Write a very catchy and fitting headline for the story.","9. Mark the copy for the printer (size, type, column, width, etc.)","10.","Encircle the slug line (short title, name of reporter),","11.","End page with a paragraph. Do not cut paragraph and continue on the next page.","12.","Write “more” at the bottom of the page of unfinished story and continue the next sheet. Do not use the back page of the paper.","13.","On the second page, instead of numbering as page 2, label it “first add” or “add one” followed by the slug. For example, “first add nabbed” or “add one nabbed”.","14.","End the story with number 30 or sharp (#). Encircle it.","15.","If the copy could hardly be read because of so many corrections, retype it if time permits to facilitate editing and typesetting.","Headline is the title of the news story. It is the display window of the newspaper. It also serves as a source of information for busy newspaper readers.","STEPS IN HEADLINE WRITING","1. Read the whole story to understand its message.","2. Underline the key words from the lead.","3. Using these key words, write a short telegraphic sentence","summary of the news (omit articles, unnecessary auxiliary verbs, prepositions, details).","4. Divide according to units of thought into the number of lines","required.","5. Substitute simple, effective synonyms to reduce width","within the limits of space allotted.","Example: Charter polls must go on, say experts RULES IN WRITING HEADLINE","1. The headline must have a verb and it must be in the present","tense Wrong: Ang Limbukad copped 5 trophies in DSPC Correct: Ang Limbukad cops 5 trophies in DSPC","2. The first letter of the first word and proper nouns are generally","capitalized.","Example: Iran offers more oil in RP","3. Do not use period.","4. Minimize punctuation marks.","Wrong: Rolling stores to sell rice and groceries Correct: Rolling stores to sell rice, groceries","5. Don’t use the articles a, an, the","Wrong: A fire hits the squatters’ area in Tondo Correct: Fire hits squatters’ area in Tondo","6. Don’t use names unless the person is well-known.","Wrong: Santos electrocuted Correct: Carpenter electrocuted","7. Use the infinitives for future events","Wrong: City Hall will push anti-squatting drive Correct: City Hall to push anti-squatting drive","8. Use specific terms instead of generalities. Wrong: School official gets","scholarship Correct: Principal gets US scholarship","9. Assertions in a headline should have a source or attribution.","Poor: Principal orders suspension of classes Strong: Classes suspended today, says principal Good: Even SC can err-Cuevas Good: Cuevas: Even SC can err","10. Use the shorter word whenever possible, e.g., use “cut” for decrease,","or “hike” for increase.","Example: School hikes Studes urge tuition fee class boycott"],"prompt":["Direction: Copyread the article below using appropriate copy reading symbols. Write the slug, printers’ direction with two headline using barline and droplines."],"example":[],"rub":[["Errors found and corrected",4],["Headlines follow the rules",4],["Slug and printer’s directions",4],["Clean, readable copy",4]],"heads":["Headline 1 (barline)","Headline 2 (droplines)"],"article":"doctor. Ma. Girlie Pinongan, provincial health officer, said there were 2,211 influenza-like cases reported from Jan. 1 to Dec. 2 this year in the 31 towns and cities of negros occidental compiared to 616 cases in 2022.\n\nhealth officials urged the public to take precautions during the holidayses as cases as cases of influenza-like illness in Negros Occidental increased by 258.93 percent this year.\n\nhe highest number of cases were recorded in Isabela town with 1,680 followed by San Carlos City with 129; Murcia, 80; Bago City, 57; Talisay City, 50; Hinigaran, 20; Cadiz City, 19; and Don Salvador Benedicto, Silay City and Victorias City with 16 each. Still, she said, the public should avoid close contact with persons manifesting influenza-like symptoms Pinongan said the cases are not severe and are usually triggered by changes in conditions weather/ She also urged people to wear maskes in closed and crowded spaces as a precaution."},{"n":11,"title":"Copyreading 2: EDC Solar Plans","kind":"copy","info":["Copyreading is editing or correcting errors made by the reporter and proofreading the errors made by the typesetter or printer.","It is the art of arranging, correcting, and selecting the quality and type of news It is also called copyediting.","One who edits copies is called a copyreader or copyeditor What to copyread?","1. Errors in fact","2. Errors in grammar","3. Errors in structure","4. Errors in style","5. Libelous and derogatory statements","6. Seditious and rebellious matter","7. Opinion, editorializing statements in news","8. Verbal deadwoods, redundancy","9. Errors in unity, coherence and emphasis","PROCEDURE IN COPYREADING","1. Read the whole article first to determine what kind of story it is.","2. Having determined the type of story, read the article again. This","time determine what the lead paragraph is.","3. Take note of errors in fact. Check on the accuracy of the","statements and information. Verify when in doubt.","4. Read the story again and mark all paragraph beginnings. Then,","correct errors in grammar, spelling, punctuations, and style using copyreading symbols.","5. Improve the organization of the story. Make sure the lead features","the lead fact.","6. Go over the whole story and again. Remove all unnecessary and","irrelevant materials.","7. Check the length of the story and then prepare the clean copy.","8. Write a very catchy and fitting headline for the story.","9. Mark the copy for the printer (size, type, column, width, etc.)","10.","Encircle the slug line (short title, name of reporter),","11.","End page with a paragraph. Do not cut paragraph and continue on the next page.","12.","Write “more” at the bottom of the page of unfinished story and continue the next sheet. Do not use the back page of the paper.","13.","On the second page, instead of numbering as page 2, label it “first add” or “add one” followed by the slug. For example, “first add nabbed” or “add one nabbed”.","14.","End the story with number 30 or sharp (#). Encircle it.","15.","If the copy could hardly be read because of so many corrections, retype it if time permits to facilitate editing and typesetting.","Headline is the title of the news story. It is the display window of the newspaper. It also serves as a source of information for busy newspaper readers.","STEPS IN HEADLINE WRITING","1. Read the whole story to understand its message.","2. Underline the key words from the lead.","3. Using these key words, write a short telegraphic sentence","summary of the news (omit articles, unnecessary auxiliary verbs, prepositions, details).","4. Divide according to units of thought into the number of lines","required.","5. Substitute simple, effective synonyms to reduce width","within the limits of space allotted.","Example: Charter polls must go on, say experts RULES IN WRITING HEADLINE","1. The headline must have a verb and it must be in the present","tense Wrong: Ang Limbukad copped 5 trophies in DSPC Correct: Ang Limbukad cops 5 trophies in DSPC","2. The first letter of the first word and proper nouns are generally","capitalized.","Example: Iran offers more oil in RP","3. Do not use period.","4. Minimize punctuation marks.","Wrong: Rolling stores to sell rice and groceries Correct: Rolling stores to sell rice, groceries","5. Don’t use the articles a, an, the","Wrong: A fire hits the squatters’ area in Tondo Correct: Fire hits squatters’ area in Tondo","6. Don’t use names unless the person is well-known.","Wrong: Santos electrocuted Correct: Carpenter electrocuted","7. Use the infinitives for future events","Wrong: City Hall will push anti-squatting drive Correct: City Hall to push anti-squatting drive","8. Use specific terms instead of generalities. Wrong: School official gets","scholarship Correct: Principal gets US scholarship","9. Assertions in a headline should have a source or attribution.","Poor: Principal orders suspension of classes Strong: Classes suspended today, says principal Good: Even SC can err-Cuevas Good: Cuevas: Even SC can err","10. Use the shorter word whenever possible, e.g., use “cut” for decrease,","or “hike” for increase.","Example: School hikes Studes urge tuition fee class boycott"],"prompt":["Direction: Copyread the article below using appropriate copy reading symbols. Write the slug, printers’ direction with two headline using crossline and flush left."],"example":[],"rub":[["Errors found and corrected",4],["Headlines follow the rules",4],["Slug and printer’s directions",4],["Clean, readable copy",4]],"heads":["Headline 1 (crossline)","Headline 2 (flush left)"],"article":"The Lopez family’s energy Developmet Corp. (edc) plans to build additional solar power plants and battery energy storage systems within its geothermal project sites to further expand its renewable energy portfolio and increase power supply across the country/ Barcena did not disclose farther details about this undertaking but noted that it wood be among the company’s priority projects in the next 2 years.\n\n“We won’t go far bcoz we already have space within our geothermal project sites. We’re looking at how we can maximize some small solar facilities inside our geothermal [power plants], Allan Barcena, EDC head of corporate social responsibility and public relations, told reporters last week.\n\nAt present, EDC has twelve megawatts (MW) of solar power capacity from two facilities in the country.\n\nApart from this, barcena said they were also looking at completing more battery energy storage system (BESS) projects in 2024 and 2025. BESS facilities are used to store excess power from power plants during off-peak hours. These are tapped for poweeer when electricity demand increases to augment supply and prevent outages.\n\n“BESS is a concept that’s evolving … I think storage will continues to be an opportunity to capture additional energy capacity, whether in hydro, solar or geothermal,” he said.\n\nEDC signed an agreement in October with First Balfour Inc., the Lopezes’ construction and engineering unit, for the the development of three BESS projects with a combined capacity of 40 MW.\n\nThese projects will be locate within EDC’s geothermal power plants in Sorsogon, Leyte and negros Oriental provinces"},{"n":12,"title":"Copyreading 3: State of Calamity","kind":"copy","info":["Copyreading is editing or correcting errors made by the reporter and proofreading the errors made by the typesetter or printer.","It is the art of arranging, correcting, and selecting the quality and type of news It is also called copyediting.","One who edits copies is called a copyreader or copyeditor What to copyread?","1. Errors in fact","2. Errors in grammar","3. Errors in structure","4. Errors in style","5. Libelous and derogatory statements","6. Seditious and rebellious matter","7. Opinion, editorializing statements in news","8. Verbal deadwoods, redundancy","9. Errors in unity, coherence and emphasis","PROCEDURE IN COPYREADING","1. Read the whole article first to determine what kind of story it is.","2. Having determined the type of story, read the article again. This","time determine what the lead paragraph is.","3. Take note of errors in fact. Check on the accuracy of the","statements and information. Verify when in doubt.","4. Read the story again and mark all paragraph beginnings. Then,","correct errors in grammar, spelling, punctuations, and style using copyreading symbols.","5. Improve the organization of the story. Make sure the lead features","the lead fact.","6. Go over the whole story and again. Remove all unnecessary and","irrelevant materials.","7. Check the length of the story and then prepare the clean copy.","8. Write a very catchy and fitting headline for the story.","9. Mark the copy for the printer (size, type, column, width, etc.)","10.","Encircle the slug line (short title, name of reporter),","11.","End page with a paragraph. Do not cut paragraph and continue on the next page.","12.","Write “more” at the bottom of the page of unfinished story and continue the next sheet. Do not use the back page of the paper.","13.","On the second page, instead of numbering as page 2, label it “first add” or “add one” followed by the slug. For example, “first add nabbed” or “add one nabbed”.","14.","End the story with number 30 or sharp (#). Encircle it.","15.","If the copy could hardly be read because of so many corrections, retype it if time permits to facilitate editing and typesetting.","Headline is the title of the news story. It is the display window of the newspaper. It also serves as a source of information for busy newspaper readers.","STEPS IN HEADLINE WRITING","1. Read the whole story to understand its message.","2. Underline the key words from the lead.","3. Using these key words, write a short telegraphic sentence","summary of the news (omit articles, unnecessary auxiliary verbs, prepositions, details).","4. Divide according to units of thought into the number of lines","required.","5. Substitute simple, effective synonyms to reduce width","within the limits of space allotted.","Example: Charter polls must go on, say experts RULES IN WRITING HEADLINE","1. The headline must have a verb and it must be in the present","tense Wrong: Ang Limbukad copped 5 trophies in DSPC Correct: Ang Limbukad cops 5 trophies in DSPC","2. The first letter of the first word and proper nouns are generally","capitalized.","Example: Iran offers more oil in RP","3. Do not use period.","4. Minimize punctuation marks.","Wrong: Rolling stores to sell rice and groceries Correct: Rolling stores to sell rice, groceries","5. Don’t use the articles a, an, the","Wrong: A fire hits the squatters’ area in Tondo Correct: Fire hits squatters’ area in Tondo","6. Don’t use names unless the person is well-known.","Wrong: Santos electrocuted Correct: Carpenter electrocuted","7. Use the infinitives for future events","Wrong: City Hall will push anti-squatting drive Correct: City Hall to push anti-squatting drive","8. Use specific terms instead of generalities. Wrong: School official gets","scholarship Correct: Principal gets US scholarship","9. Assertions in a headline should have a source or attribution.","Poor: Principal orders suspension of classes Strong: Classes suspended today, says principal Good: Even SC can err-Cuevas Good: Cuevas: Even SC can err","10. Use the shorter word whenever possible, e.g., use “cut” for decrease,","or “hike” for increase.","Example: School hikes Studes urge tuition fee class boycott"],"prompt":["Direction: Copyread the article below using appropriate copy reading symbols. Write the slug, printers’ direction with two headline using crossline and inverted pyramid."],"example":[],"rub":[["Errors found and corrected",4],["Headlines follow the rules",4],["Slug and printer’s directions",4],["Clean, readable copy",4]],"heads":["Headline 1 (crossline)","Headline 2 (inverted pyramid)"],"article":"The provincial cuncil issued Resolution No. 1410-23 on wednesday, declaring a state of calamity, following Hinatuan town’s similar declaration the previous day The provincial gov’t emphasized declaring a state ofcalamity is a crucial step in aiding the community after the temblor.\n\nThe provincial government of surigao del Sur hasplaced the entire province under a state of calamity after a powerful magnitude 7.4 earthquake struck off its coasts onsSaturday evening (December 2).\n\nsurigao del Sur’s Provincial Disaster reduction Risk and Office management said more than 30 percent of the province’s population was affected by the earthquake.\n\nData from the National Disaster Risk reduction and ManagementCouncil (ndrrmc) showed as of 8 p.m. on Tuesday, the quake reportedly killed 3 and injured 48 individuals NDRRMC has logged 3,336 aftershocks from the magnitude 7.4 tremor as of 4 p.m. the same day.\n\n“Be it resolved that by the Panlalawigan Sangguniang of Surigao del Sur to diclare the entire province of Surigao del Sur under State of Calamity due to the effects of the magnitude 7.4 offshore earthquake that struck the Province on december 2, 20 23 and the subsequent recurring tremors and big aftershocks,” the resolution read"},{"n":13,"title":"Student Inventors: Headline and Lead","kind":"write","info":["Types of Science Article(According to structure)","Science News Science Feature Science Editorial Elements of Science Article Definition of the Word Case Study Statistics Expert’s Quote Important Points for Science Editorial S – State the problem P – Position on the problem E – Evidence to support your position C – Conclusion (Who’s going to be affected and how?)","S – Solutions to the problem SOME TIPS IN WRITING SCIENCE STORIES Write LEAN Clear, concise language Easy on the adjectives Use short sentences/paragraphs 800-1,000 words As high as 1,500 words if the stories SOME TIPS IN WRITING SCIENCE STORIES","1. AVOID JARGON - When","using unfamiliar as briefly as possible. Give its literal meaning.","scientific terms, define","2. MAKE IT DESCRIPTIVE; GIVE IT A TOUCH OF COLOR","3. EMPHASIZE THE MAGNITUDE OF THE PROBLEM","4. GIVE THE OTHER SIDE OF THE SITUATION","Widen the discussion to include other aspects of the problem or situation","5. CITE YOUR SOURCE, MOST ESPECIALLY IN CONTROVERSIAL ISSUES","6. HUMANIZE YOUR STORY","Inject emotional element Interesting experience, milestone, path- breaking success, heartbreaking failures Use quotes & dialogues","7. MAKE YOUR LEADS INTERESTING","They seek out and find each other and mate. Then they remain locked in continuous sexual intercourse for 20-30 years."],"prompt":["Read the selection below and answer the questions that follow: STUDENT INVENTORS Three senior high school students developed a low-cost mobile app that detects early signs of dengue outbreaks using location-based reports and temperature data."],"example":[],"sections":[{"l":"A. Headline (6–10 words)","h":"","rows":1},{"l":"B. Lead (1–2 sentences)","h":"","rows":3},{"l":"Reflection","h":"Why is it important for journalists to write clear and accurate headlines and leads in science and technology news?","rows":4}],"rub":[["Content and ideas",4],["Organization",4],["Language and style",4],["Mechanics (spelling, grammar, punctuation)",4]]},{"n":14,"title":"Background of the Task: Context and Body","kind":"write","info":["Types of Science Article(According to structure)","Science News Science Feature Science Editorial Elements of Science Article Definition of the Word Case Study Statistics Expert’s Quote Important Points for Science Editorial S – State the problem P – Position on the problem E – Evidence to support your position C – Conclusion (Who’s going to be affected and how?)","S – Solutions to the problem SOME TIPS IN WRITING SCIENCE STORIES Write LEAN Clear, concise language Easy on the adjectives Use short sentences/paragraphs 800-1,000 words As high as 1,500 words if the stories SOME TIPS IN WRITING SCIENCE STORIES","1. AVOID JARGON - When","using unfamiliar as briefly as possible. Give its literal meaning.","scientific terms, define","2. MAKE IT DESCRIPTIVE; GIVE IT A TOUCH OF COLOR","3. EMPHASIZE THE MAGNITUDE OF THE PROBLEM","4. GIVE THE OTHER SIDE OF THE SITUATION","Widen the discussion to include other aspects of the problem or situation","5. CITE YOUR SOURCE, MOST ESPECIALLY IN CONTROVERSIAL ISSUES","6. HUMANIZE YOUR STORY","Inject emotional element Interesting experience, milestone, path- breaking success, heartbreaking failures Use quotes & dialogues","7. MAKE YOUR LEADS INTERESTING","They seek out and find each other and mate. Then they remain locked in continuous sexual intercourse for 20-30 years."],"prompt":["Background of the Task You are a student journalist assigned to write a technology news article. Your task is to develop the context and main body content of the story based on the information below:","News Scenario:","Three senior high school students developed a low-cost mobile app that detects early signs of dengue outbreaks using location-based reports and temperature data.","Using the news scenario above, complete the tasks below:"],"example":[],"sections":[{"l":"A. Context paragraph (3–4 sentences)","h":"Background of the dengue issue and why a detection app is important.","rows":5},{"l":"B. Main body paragraph (4–6 sentences)","h":"How the app works, who created it, its possible impact. You may include invented quotes.","rows":7},{"l":"Reflection","h":"How can technology created by students contribute to solving real community problems?","rows":4}],"rub":[["Content and ideas",4],["Organization",4],["Language and style",4],["Mechanics (spelling, grammar, punctuation)",4]]},{"n":15,"title":"Science and Technology: Supporting Details","kind":"write","info":["Types of Science Article(According to structure)","Science News Science Feature Science Editorial Elements of Science Article Definition of the Word Case Study Statistics Expert’s Quote Important Points for Science Editorial S – State the problem P – Position on the problem E – Evidence to support your position C – Conclusion (Who’s going to be affected and how?)","S – Solutions to the problem SOME TIPS IN WRITING SCIENCE STORIES Write LEAN Clear, concise language Easy on the adjectives Use short sentences/paragraphs 800-1,000 words As high as 1,500 words if the stories SOME TIPS IN WRITING SCIENCE STORIES","1. AVOID JARGON - When","using unfamiliar as briefly as possible. Give its literal meaning.","scientific terms, define","2. MAKE IT DESCRIPTIVE; GIVE IT A TOUCH OF COLOR","3. EMPHASIZE THE MAGNITUDE OF THE PROBLEM","4. GIVE THE OTHER SIDE OF THE SITUATION","Widen the discussion to include other aspects of the problem or situation","5. CITE YOUR SOURCE, MOST ESPECIALLY IN CONTROVERSIAL ISSUES","6. HUMANIZE YOUR STORY","Inject emotional element Interesting experience, milestone, path- breaking success, heartbreaking failures Use quotes & dialogues","7. MAKE YOUR LEADS INTERESTING","They seek out and find each other and mate. Then they remain locked in continuous sexual intercourse for 20-30 years."],"prompt":["Scenario:","Three senior high school students developed a low-cost mobile app that detects early signs of dengue outbreaks using location-based reports and temperature data."],"example":[],"sections":[{"l":"Additional details (3–4 sentences)","h":"The app, dengue data, student motivations, or expert opinions.","rows":5},{"l":"Significance (2–3 sentences)","h":"The innovation’s potential impact on the community or the future.","rows":4},{"l":"Reflection","h":"How does supporting information make a news article more reliable and informative?","rows":4}],"rub":[["Content and ideas",4],["Organization",4],["Language and style",4],["Mechanics (spelling, grammar, punctuation)",4]]},{"n":16,"title":"A Day in the Life of…","kind":"write","info":["Feature writing is an art that combines factual reporting with creative storytelling to produce an engaging and informative piece. By following these guidelines, writers can craft stories that resonate with their audience, providing depth and insight into a wide range of topics.","Key Components","1. Headline: Captivating and summarizing the essence of the","story.","2. Lead (Lede): An opening paragraph that hooks the reader, often","with an anecdote, striking fact, or question.","3. Nut Graf: A paragraph that outlines the central theme or","purpose of the story, providing a roadmap for what’s ahead.","4. Body:","Background Information: Essential details that set the scene or provide context.","Development: The story’s main content, including arguments, descriptions, and narratives.","Supporting Elements: Quotes, statistics, anecdotes, and examples that enrich the narrative.","5. Conclusion: Wraps up the story, often tying back to the lead","or looking forward to the future.","6. Call to Action: (Optional) Directs readers toward","further engagement or exploration of the topic."],"prompt":["A Day in the Life of ….","1. Choose someone to feature: a classmate, teacher, family member,","neighbor, or even a school staff member (e.g., librarian, guard, janitor).","2. Interview the person using at least 5 questions (e.g., daily","routine, hobbies, challenges, fun facts).","3. Write a 3-4 paragraph feature article titled “A Day in the Life of","_______.”","4. Focus on capturing interesting details, personality, and real","experiences.","5. Add one photo or hand-drawn illustration (optional)."],"example":["Title: A Day in the Life of Mr. Santos, the School Guard","Mr. Santos steps through the school gate before the sun has fully risen, his uniform crisp and his flashlight in hand. He smiles warmly as he greets the early students trickling in, locking the gate behind him. “I like being the first one here,” he says. “It gives me time to think and prepare for the day.”","Throughout the morning, he patrols the corridors, making sure classrooms are secure and reminding students to put their things away. When someone forgets a book or backpack, he helps them carry it to their classroom. At recess, he watches students play, sometimes pausing to chat he asks about their day, what subjects they like, and even what games they play at home.","In the afternoon, Mr. Santos helps guide students out of the school grounds safely. He stands by the gate, waving goodbye, and makes sure no one lags behind. After the last student leaves, he walks around to check every door and window. He says, “My job isn’t just to guard the school — I guard their memories too. I want them to leave knowing they're safe.” As he closes up, his face shows a quiet pride: even in the small moments, he knows he’s made a difference."],"sections":[{"l":"Who are you featuring?","h":"","rows":1},{"l":"Interview questions and answers (at least 5)","h":"","rows":8},{"l":"A Day in the Life of … (3–4 paragraphs)","h":"","rows":14}],"rub":[["Content and ideas",4],["Organization",4],["Language and style",4],["Mechanics (spelling, grammar, punctuation)",4]]},{"n":17,"title":"The Secret Garden Behind the Science Lab","kind":"write","info":["Feature writing is an art that combines factual reporting with creative storytelling to produce an engaging and informative piece. By following these guidelines, writers can craft stories that resonate with their audience, providing depth and insight into a wide range of topics.","Key Components","1. Headline: Captivating and summarizing the essence of the","story.","2. Lead (Lede): An opening paragraph that hooks the reader, often","with an anecdote, striking fact, or question.","3. Nut Graf: A paragraph that outlines the central theme or","purpose of the story, providing a roadmap for what’s ahead.","4. Body:","Background Information: Essential details that set the scene or provide context.","Development: The story’s main content, including arguments, descriptions, and narratives.","Supporting Elements: Quotes, statistics, anecdotes, and examples that enrich the narrative.","5. Conclusion: Wraps up the story, often tying back to the lead","or looking forward to the future.","6. Call to Action: (Optional) Directs readers toward","further engagement or exploration of the topic."],"prompt":["Instructions:","1. Explore the school campus and identify a “hidden gem”—a place","or feature students may overlook (e.g., a quiet reading corner, an art display, a garden spot).","2. Describe what makes this place special or unique.","3. Interview at least 1 person who knows something about the","location (e.g., librarian, teacher, classmate).","4. Write a short feature article (2–3 paragraphs) that includes","descriptive details, sensory imagery, and interesting facts.","5. Give the article a catchy title (e.g., “The Secret Garden Behind the","Science Lab”)."],"example":["Title: The Secret Garden Behind the Science Lab","Paragraph","– Description:","Tucked behind the science lab, away from the bustling hallways, lies a small garden that most students pass by without noticing. Sunlight filters through the branches of a tall jacaranda tree, casting dappled shadows on the neatly arranged flowerbeds. The air is fragrant with the scent of blooming marigolds and jasmine, and the gentle sound of rustling leaves creates a peaceful atmosphere. A worn wooden bench sits under the tree, inviting anyone to pause and enjoy the quiet.","Paragraph","– Stories and Facts:","Mrs. Alvarez, the school’s biology teacher, explains that the garden was planted by students five years ago as part of a sustainability project. “It started as a simple idea to grow flowers and herbs for the school, but it became a place where students could relax and observe nature,” she says. Over the years, students have added bird feeders, a small pond, and colorful stepping stones. Many report that spending time here helps them focus and feel calm between classes.","Paragraph","– Reflection / Significance:","Though it may seem small and unassuming, this garden is a hidden treasure in the school. It reminds everyone that even in a busy campus, there are spaces to pause, reflect, and connect with nature. For those who take the time to explore it, the garden offers more than beauty—it offers a moment of calm and inspiration in the middle of a hectic school day."],"sections":[{"l":"Catchy title","h":"","rows":1},{"l":"Feature article (2–3 paragraphs)","h":"Description · stories and facts · reflection.","rows":14}],"rub":[["Content and ideas",4],["Organization",4],["Language and style",4],["Mechanics (spelling, grammar, punctuation)",4]]},{"n":18,"title":"The Old Trophy from the First Math Contest","kind":"write","info":["Feature writing is an art that combines factual reporting with creative storytelling to produce an engaging and informative piece. By following these guidelines, writers can craft stories that resonate with their audience, providing depth and insight into a wide range of topics.","Key Components","1. Headline: Captivating and summarizing the essence of the","story.","2. Lead (Lede): An opening paragraph that hooks the reader, often","with an anecdote, striking fact, or question.","3. Nut Graf: A paragraph that outlines the central theme or","purpose of the story, providing a roadmap for what’s ahead.","4. Body:","Background Information: Essential details that set the scene or provide context.","Development: The story’s main content, including arguments, descriptions, and narratives.","Supporting Elements: Quotes, statistics, anecdotes, and examples that enrich the narrative.","5. Conclusion: Wraps up the story, often tying back to the lead","or looking forward to the future.","6. Call to Action: (Optional) Directs readers toward","further engagement or exploration of the topic."],"prompt":["1. Bring an object from home or choose one from the classroom (e.g.,","a favorite book, an old photo, a trophy, a toy).","2. Research or reflect on the object’s background:","Where did it come from?","Why is it meaningful?","What memories or events are connected to it?","3. Write a feature-style narrative (3 paragraphs) that tells the","“story” of the object in an interesting way.","4. Use sensory details and emotion to make the object come alive."],"example":["Title: The Old Trophy from the First Math Contest","In the trophy case near the principal’s office, there is an old silver cup decorated with ribbons. The plaque reads: “First Inter-School Math Contest – 1985.” It’s a little dusty now, but when you hold it, you can feel its history.","Mr. Reyes, one of the oldest teachers, remembers when the school won that first contest. “We practiced every day after school,” he recalls. “We were so poor at first, but we really wanted to prove we could compete.” That math contest was a big deal — students from different schools came, and our school didn’t expect to win. But they did, and when they lifted that trophy, everyone cheered.","Now, the trophy stays in the case. Students look at it and talk about who came before them, and what it took. For some, it’s just a shiny cup. For others, it’s a symbol: with hard work, even ordinary students can do extraordinary things."],"sections":[{"l":"Title","h":"","rows":1},{"l":"The story of the object (3 paragraphs)","h":"Sensory details and emotion.","rows":14}],"rub":[["Content and ideas",4],["Organization",4],["Language and style",4],["Mechanics (spelling, grammar, punctuation)",4]]},{"n":19,"title":"Parts of a Column","kind":"match","info":["Columns are articles or features written for newspapers, magazines, newsletters, and other publications. They are usually published regularly and on a schedule. Columns are a form of journalism that is less formal and more biased than other types of journalism. Choose the content you want your column to be about and then write your column following simple guidelines.","When writing a column, do","• Give the reader timely, helpful information.","• Develop a structure and keep it. Write on a regular schedule.","• Write simple and short sentences and paragraphs.","• In personal columns, use local names and places.","• Let others speak for you by use of quotes and references.","• Learn the difference between a column and a news story.","Tips on column writing","• Write the way you talk. But don't discard good English usage","and grammar.","• Try to uncover a \"lead\" or opening that will catch the interest","of your readers.","• Use a variety of material, not just one subject.","• Write about people. When using subject matter, try to tell the","story through the experiences of local people.","• Write simply. Avoid technical or difficult words, long","sentences, long paragraphs.","• Don't weigh your column down with too much detail. Try to","stimulate interest in a subject, but don't exhaust the subject.","• Jot down ideas, names, figures, impressions, etc., in a note","pad while visiting farms and homes. This provides the very best column material.","• Be timely. Keep up with the effect of weather conditions,","seasons, etc., pointing out the significance of these conditions locally.","• Remember the people you're talking to and give them","information that will benefit them in a way they can understand.","• Always get your column to the editor or publication on","schedule."],"prompt":["Match each part of a column to what it does."],"example":[],"pairs":[["Title / Headline","The name of the column — catchy and shows the topic."],["Introduction / Lead","The opening paragraph that introduces the topic and grabs attention."],["Opinion / Viewpoint","The writer’s personal opinion or stance on the topic."],["Body / Supporting details","Paragraphs that explain, describe or give examples to support the opinion."],["Conclusion / Closing","The final paragraph that sums up and reinforces the opinion."]]},{"n":20,"title":"Column Writing: Put It in Order","kind":"order","info":["Columns are articles or features written for newspapers, magazines, newsletters, and other publications. They are usually published regularly and on a schedule. Columns are a form of journalism that is less formal and more biased than other types of journalism. Choose the content you want your column to be about and then write your column following simple guidelines.","When writing a column, do","• Give the reader timely, helpful information.","• Develop a structure and keep it. Write on a regular schedule.","• Write simple and short sentences and paragraphs.","• In personal columns, use local names and places.","• Let others speak for you by use of quotes and references.","• Learn the difference between a column and a news story.","Tips on column writing","• Write the way you talk. But don't discard good English usage","and grammar.","• Try to uncover a \"lead\" or opening that will catch the interest","of your readers.","• Use a variety of material, not just one subject.","• Write about people. When using subject matter, try to tell the","story through the experiences of local people.","• Write simply. Avoid technical or difficult words, long","sentences, long paragraphs.","• Don't weigh your column down with too much detail. Try to","stimulate interest in a subject, but don't exhaust the subject.","• Jot down ideas, names, figures, impressions, etc., in a note","pad while visiting farms and homes. This provides the very best column material.","• Be timely. Keep up with the effect of weather conditions,","seasons, etc., pointing out the significance of these conditions locally.","• Remember the people you're talking to and give them","information that will benefit them in a way they can understand.","• Always get your column to the editor or publication on","schedule."],"prompt":["Put the parts of the column in the correct order: title, introduction, opinion, body, conclusion."],"example":[],"items":["School Sports: More Than Just Games","Sports are an important part of school life because they help students stay active and healthy. They also make school more enjoyable through fun and energetic activities.","I believe Grade 7 students should join school sports because they learn more than just playing the game. Sports teach teamwork, discipline, and responsibility.","Students who participate in sports also build confidence through regular practice. They learn to cooperate with teammates and value hard work.","In conclusion, school sports help students grow physically, mentally, and socially. They build character and prepare young people for future challenges."],"labels":["Title","Introduction","Opinion","Body","Conclusion"]},{"n":21,"title":"My Favorite Hobby","kind":"write","info":["Columns are articles or features written for newspapers, magazines, newsletters, and other publications. They are usually published regularly and on a schedule. Columns are a form of journalism that is less formal and more biased than other types of journalism. Choose the content you want your column to be about and then write your column following simple guidelines.","When writing a column, do","• Give the reader timely, helpful information.","• Develop a structure and keep it. Write on a regular schedule.","• Write simple and short sentences and paragraphs.","• In personal columns, use local names and places.","• Let others speak for you by use of quotes and references.","• Learn the difference between a column and a news story.","Tips on column writing","• Write the way you talk. But don't discard good English usage","and grammar.","• Try to uncover a \"lead\" or opening that will catch the interest","of your readers.","• Use a variety of material, not just one subject.","• Write about people. When using subject matter, try to tell the","story through the experiences of local people.","• Write simply. Avoid technical or difficult words, long","sentences, long paragraphs.","• Don't weigh your column down with too much detail. Try to","stimulate interest in a subject, but don't exhaust the subject.","• Jot down ideas, names, figures, impressions, etc., in a note","pad while visiting farms and homes. This provides the very best column material.","• Be timely. Keep up with the effect of weather conditions,","seasons, etc., pointing out the significance of these conditions locally.","• Remember the people you're talking to and give them","information that will benefit them in a way they can understand.","• Always get your column to the editor or publication on","schedule."],"prompt":["Write a short column or article about “My Favorite Hobby”. Write each part in its own box: headline, introduction, body and conclusion."],"example":[],"sections":[{"l":"Headline","h":"","rows":1},{"l":"Introduction","h":"","rows":4},{"l":"Body","h":"","rows":7},{"l":"Conclusion","h":"","rows":3}],"rub":[["Content and ideas",4],["Organization",4],["Language and style",4],["Mechanics (spelling, grammar, punctuation)",4]]},{"n":22,"title":"River in Crisis: Editorial Cartoon","kind":"draw","info":["Editorial Cartooning Cartooning is an illustration that shows humorous events of which may either be of a serious one or not. Cartooning comes from two words which are Caricature and Lampoon.","Caricature is an exaggerated illustration of someone or something by enlarging some body parts making it look funny.","Lampoon is a writing which publicly criticizes someone or something. Editorial Cartoon (also called Political Cartoon) is an opinionated drawing of some political or social issues. It may either be done to attack, criticize, entertain or praise. It is usually partnered with the editorial and may also be seen in features, literary, etc. when there is no appropriate picture to fit it.","DOs Do deal with a single topic and be original.","Do have humor and be entertaining if possible.","Do use symbols which are generally known by people in order to avoid confusion.","Drawings that represents something.","Ex. A crocodile represents a greedy person or corrupt politician. Do have less texts like captions, labels, and dialogs.","Do have a balance between the artwork created and the opinion of the cartoonist.","Don’t over-exaggerate so as not to make it look corny.","Don’t or avoid hurting someone.","Don’t attack the person himself instead of his actions.","Don’t use several illustrations for it may confuse the reader. Around 3 – 5 illustrations will do.","DONTs STEPS IN DOING EDITORIAL CARTOONING The steps in doing an editorial cartoon:","1. Issue","Know the issue you are concerned with and know the facts related to it.","2. Stand","Questions like “Which side do you agree or disagree with?” and “What elements affect the issue?”","3. Sketching","The creation of the guide or draft of your cartoon.","4. Outlining/Sketching","The detailed part of the cartoon is created by using a pen to trace the shape of the sketch.","5. Shading","An optional step where in you give depth to your drawing making it more realistic."],"prompt":["Draw a cartoon showing how the river looks now, full of plastic waste. Include fish, humans, and trash in a visual story:","Fish struggling or sick from microplastics.","Mang Tonyo trying to catch fish in trash-filled nets.","Residents or children holding signs: “Save Maliit River!”","“River in Crisis: Plastic Waste Threatens Local Fish Population” San Isidro, Laguna — Residents of Barangay Malinaw were shocked this week after local scientists released a report revealing dangerously high levels of plastic waste in the Maliit River, a waterway once known for its clear water and abundant fish.","According to the team of researchers from Laguna State Environmental Laboratory, plastic pollution in the river has increased by 63% in the last five years. The most concerning part of the study shows that if the trend continues, the community may lose up to 60% of its fish population in the next five years.","“We found plastic bags, sachets, straws, and even broken toys trapped in the riverbed,” said Dr. Elisa Ramos, lead scientist of the study. “Fish are mistaking microplastics for food. Many are getting sick or dying.” Fisherfolk in the area have also noticed the alarming decline. Mang Tonyo, a fisherman of 30 years, shared that his usual three kilos of fish per day has dropped to less than one kilo. “Minsan wala pa nga. Puno ng basura ang lambat namin,” he said, showing nets tangled with plastic waste.","Local officials admitted that the river cleanup programs have not been enough. Barangay Captain Lucia Hernandez urged residents to reduce single-use plastics and participate in the upcoming community cleanup next month. Environmental groups warn that without immediate action, the river may become biologically dead—unable to support fish or other aquatic life.","“This river feeds families,” Dr. Ramos added. “If we lose it, we lose part of our community.”"],"example":[],"rub":[["Shows the issue clearly",4],["Symbols and labels",4],["Creativity",4],["Neat and readable",4]],"caption":true},{"n":23,"title":"Luna and the Sad River: Cartoon","kind":"draw","info":["Editorial Cartooning Cartooning is an illustration that shows humorous events of which may either be of a serious one or not. Cartooning comes from two words which are Caricature and Lampoon.","Caricature is an exaggerated illustration of someone or something by enlarging some body parts making it look funny.","Lampoon is a writing which publicly criticizes someone or something. Editorial Cartoon (also called Political Cartoon) is an opinionated drawing of some political or social issues. It may either be done to attack, criticize, entertain or praise. It is usually partnered with the editorial and may also be seen in features, literary, etc. when there is no appropriate picture to fit it.","DOs Do deal with a single topic and be original.","Do have humor and be entertaining if possible.","Do use symbols which are generally known by people in order to avoid confusion.","Drawings that represents something.","Ex. A crocodile represents a greedy person or corrupt politician. Do have less texts like captions, labels, and dialogs.","Do have a balance between the artwork created and the opinion of the cartoonist.","Don’t over-exaggerate so as not to make it look corny.","Don’t or avoid hurting someone.","Don’t attack the person himself instead of his actions.","Don’t use several illustrations for it may confuse the reader. Around 3 – 5 illustrations will do.","DONTs STEPS IN DOING EDITORIAL CARTOONING The steps in doing an editorial cartoon:","1. Issue","Know the issue you are concerned with and know the facts related to it.","2. Stand","Questions like “Which side do you agree or disagree with?” and “What elements affect the issue?”","3. Sketching","The creation of the guide or draft of your cartoon.","4. Outlining/Sketching","The detailed part of the cartoon is created by using a pen to trace the shape of the sketch.","5. Shading","An optional step where in you give depth to your drawing making it more realistic."],"prompt":["Luna and the Sad River","In the town of Malinaw, there flowed a river that everyone loved. It was called the Maliit River, and it sparkled under the sun like a long, silver ribbon. Children played by its shore, fishermen caught fresh fish every morning, and families enjoyed picnics on weekends.","But one day, Luna, a curious 12-year-old girl, noticed something different.","When she sat by the river, she frowned.","“Why is the water… darker?” she whispered.","Instead of smooth stones and shiny fish, Luna saw plastic bags, sachets, straws, and even an old broken toy floating in the water. The fish looked unhappy too. One small fish swam near her, bumping into bits of plastic.","“Oh no,” Luna said. “Are you okay?”","The fish couldn’t speak, of course, but Luna imagined it saying, “We’re trying… but the trash is making it hard to breathe.”","That afternoon, Luna went to visit Mang Tonyo, the friendly fisherman who always told funny stories. She saw him pulling up his fishing net, but instead of fish, it was full of plastic.","“Mang Tonyo, what happened?” Luna asked.","He sighed. “The river is sick, anak. There’s too much trash. I used to catch three kilos of fish every day. Now… sometimes I catch none.” Luna felt a tight squeeze in her chest. How could a river get sick? The next day at school, Dr. Ramos, a scientist from the community, visited their class.","“Children,” she said gently, “we studied the Maliit River, and we found that plastic waste has increased a lot. If nothing changes, we might lose more than half of the fish in just a few years.”","The whole class gasped.","“But it’s not too late,” Dr. Ramos continued with a hopeful smile. “If we work together, we can help the river heal.”","Luna’s eyes brightened. She had an idea.","That weekend, with help from her classmates, she organized a River Rescue Day. They brought gloves, sacks, and posters that said, “A Clean River is a Happy River!” Even parents and neighbors joined in. They picked up trash, sorted recyclables, and planted new plants along the riverbank. It was hard work, but Luna felt proud.","At the end of the day, she sat by the river again. This time, she saw something wonderful—small fish swimming freely in a cleaner spot. Luna “Don’t worry, little river,” she whispered. “We’re here to help.” smiled.","And from that day on, the people of Malinaw promised to take better care of the Maliit River—so it could sparkle once more and stay alive for future generations.","Task:","Draw a cartoon showing Luna discovering the polluted river.","Include:","Dark, plastic-filled water Sad or struggling fish Luna’s worried expression Optional: A thought bubble for the fish: “We’re trying… but the trash is making it hard to breathe.”"],"example":[],"rub":[["Shows the issue clearly",4],["Symbols and labels",4],["Creativity",4],["Neat and readable",4]],"caption":true},{"n":24,"title":"Cell Phones in Class: Cartoon","kind":"draw","info":["Editorial Cartooning Cartooning is an illustration that shows humorous events of which may either be of a serious one or not. Cartooning comes from two words which are Caricature and Lampoon.","Caricature is an exaggerated illustration of someone or something by enlarging some body parts making it look funny.","Lampoon is a writing which publicly criticizes someone or something. Editorial Cartoon (also called Political Cartoon) is an opinionated drawing of some political or social issues. It may either be done to attack, criticize, entertain or praise. It is usually partnered with the editorial and may also be seen in features, literary, etc. when there is no appropriate picture to fit it.","DOs Do deal with a single topic and be original.","Do have humor and be entertaining if possible.","Do use symbols which are generally known by people in order to avoid confusion.","Drawings that represents something.","Ex. A crocodile represents a greedy person or corrupt politician. Do have less texts like captions, labels, and dialogs.","Do have a balance between the artwork created and the opinion of the cartoonist.","Don’t over-exaggerate so as not to make it look corny.","Don’t or avoid hurting someone.","Don’t attack the person himself instead of his actions.","Don’t use several illustrations for it may confuse the reader. Around 3 – 5 illustrations will do.","DONTs STEPS IN DOING EDITORIAL CARTOONING The steps in doing an editorial cartoon:","1. Issue","Know the issue you are concerned with and know the facts related to it.","2. Stand","Questions like “Which side do you agree or disagree with?” and “What elements affect the issue?”","3. Sketching","The creation of the guide or draft of your cartoon.","4. Outlining/Sketching","The detailed part of the cartoon is created by using a pen to trace the shape of the sketch.","5. Shading","An optional step where in you give depth to your drawing making it more realistic."],"prompt":["Article for Students","Title:","Schools Debate Cell Phone Use in Class Several schools in the region are debating whether to ban cell phones during class. Teachers say phones distract students and cause lower performance. Students argue that phones help them research quickly and communicate during emergencies. Some schools are considering a “limited use” policy to balance both sides.","Create a cartoon showing both advantages and disadvantages of cell phones in school.","Include at least two characters."],"example":[],"rub":[["Shows the issue clearly",4],["Symbols and labels",4],["Creativity",4],["Neat and readable",4]],"caption":true},{"n":25,"title":"Radio: Show Greeting","kind":"air","info":["Radio broadcasting is a hot media. Hot in the sense that it brings news immediately, right as it is happening, also because it is a man or woman talking to other men other women, be they young, adult or old. It is a voice. But one must tread carefully when it comes to fastness. One should never spread neither rumor nor hearsay. History has shown us just how far disrespecting exactness and objectivity can lead.","The principles of Radio Broadcasting are:","1. On-air Delivery – the right tone","2. Respecting Others","3. Accuracy","4. Balance","5. Clarity","6. No Commenting","7. Protect Your Sources","8. Keep in Touch with the Newsroom"],"prompt":["Instructions: Write a 10-15 second greeting that a radio host might say at the start of a show. Write your greeting in the space provided below. Requirements:","A warm greeting Station name A short welcome message"],"example":["“Good morning! You’re listening to One Voice News Radio, bringing you the latest news and updates all day! Stay tuned for today’s top stories. We’ve got important reports and interviews lined up just for you!” Write your greeting here:"],"check":["A warm greeting","Station name","A short welcome message"],"rub":[["Warm, right on-air tone",4],["Clear pronunciation",4],["Pace and timing",4],["Complete content",4]],"secs":[10,15]},{"n":26,"title":"Radio: Weather Report","kind":"air","info":["Radio broadcasting is a hot media. Hot in the sense that it brings news immediately, right as it is happening, also because it is a man or woman talking to other men other women, be they young, adult or old. It is a voice. But one must tread carefully when it comes to fastness. One should never spread neither rumor nor hearsay. History has shown us just how far disrespecting exactness and objectivity can lead.","The principles of Radio Broadcasting are:","1. On-air Delivery – the right tone","2. Respecting Others","3. Accuracy","4. Balance","5. Clarity","6. No Commenting","7. Protect Your Sources","8. Keep in Touch with the Newsroom"],"prompt":["Instructions: Write a short weather report about the information provided below.","Greeting Today’s weather Temperature A simple forecast (e.g. “Sunny this afternoon”, “cloudy skies”) Advice for listeners (e.g. “Don’t forget your jacket!”)"],"example":["“Good day, everyone! Today in Bayabason, Maramag, Bukidnon, expect cloudy skies with temperatures around 22 degrees. Don’t forget to bring your umbrellas, be safe!","Write your report here:"],"check":["Greeting","Today’s weather","Temperature","A simple forecast","Advice for listeners"],"rub":[["Warm, right on-air tone",4],["Clear pronunciation",4],["Pace and timing",4],["Complete content",4]],"secs":[15,30]},{"n":27,"title":"Radio: School Announcement","kind":"air","info":["Radio broadcasting is a hot media. Hot in the sense that it brings news immediately, right as it is happening, also because it is a man or woman talking to other men other women, be they young, adult or old. It is a voice. But one must tread carefully when it comes to fastness. One should never spread neither rumor nor hearsay. History has shown us just how far disrespecting exactness and objectivity can lead.","The principles of Radio Broadcasting are:","1. On-air Delivery – the right tone","2. Respecting Others","3. Accuracy","4. Balance","5. Clarity","6. No Commenting","7. Protect Your Sources","8. Keep in Touch with the Newsroom"],"prompt":["Instructions: Write a short school announcement about an upcoming event (field trip, contest, club meeting), or a reminder (uniform, deadlines, project submissions).","Requirements:","Greeting What the announcement is about Important details (date, time, place)","A short closing line"],"example":["“Good afternoon, students! Don’t forget that the Art Club meets tomorrow at 3 p.m. in Room 12. Everyone is welcome to join. This is FIS News Radio reminding you to stay creative!”","Write your short school announcement script here:"],"check":["Greeting","What the announcement is about","Date, time and place","A short closing line"],"rub":[["Warm, right on-air tone",4],["Clear pronunciation",4],["Pace and timing",4],["Complete content",4]],"secs":[15,30]}];
